        "text": "Blockchain, though, is a public database. Everyone needs to be able to look at a transaction and say with certainty that it's valid. If someone adds data that says Alice gives 50,000 coins to Bob, how can we tell that Alice actually authorized the transaction? Who's to say that Bob didn't just add it himself? Maybe you could have some kind of a password system, but that gives you two either you use a centralized entity to check them, or Alice publishes her password for every transaction. The former doesn't work in a decentralized setting. You might as well just use a regular database. The latter doesn't work because everyone would know Alice's password after her first transaction. Anyone could make a transaction masquerading as her.",
        "start": 160,
        "end": 44700,
        "confidence": 0.9822766,
